WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
“MILLIONAIRES”:
A few weeks ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:06.660
I made a video about big tech as I tend to do&nbsp;
and somewhere in the video I mentioned that FAANG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:11.340
engineers generally have enough money to retire&nbsp;
by the time they’re in their 30s. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.500
they tend to cross the $200,000 salary mark pretty&nbsp;
early in their career and by the time they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:22.560
been in the workforce for 10 years, their initial&nbsp;
stock compensation itself has grown 5 to 10X. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.840
it doesn’t take them long to become a millionaire&nbsp;
or even a multi millionaire. To this, an Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.060
engineer commented that retiring by 30 was the&nbsp;
funniest line in the video. He went on to claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.980
that he had been working at Apple for almost 10&nbsp;
years now and that he was nowhere near retirement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.320
We had a bit of back and forth after this but&nbsp;
he eventually said this. “I'm not a manager.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.120
But I'm in the highest tier of Software Engineer.&nbsp;
Just to give you a sense of my total compensation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.760
I pay six figures in federal taxes per year.&nbsp;
(And I still have to pay California tax on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.560
of that.) I'm a multi-millionnaire. I own a house.&nbsp;
I own more than 1 million worth of US$ of Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.500
stocks. But here is the thing. It doesn't make&nbsp;
me rich in Silicon Valley. I'm still very middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.800
class. The cost of life is so high.” It might be&nbsp;
easy to look at this and be like: Oh, it’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.300
another multi millionaire complaining about how&nbsp;
they don’t have enough money but I think there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.700
a lot more layers to this. For one, I think this&nbsp;
engineer is very much genuine in that he doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.980
feel comfortable retiring in California even&nbsp;
with a multi million dollar net worth. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:31.020
statistically speaking, being a milllioniare&nbsp;
really isn’t all that special nowadays. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.520
having a million dollar net worth somehow&nbsp;
doesn’t even get you into the top 10% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.180
households within America and that was as of&nbsp;
2020. Stocks have gone insane since then and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.880
given that the wealthiest 10% of Americans own&nbsp;
89% of the market, they made a killing. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:53.160
being a millionaire may only put you within&nbsp;
the top 20% of Americans. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:59.100
nearly ⅕ Americans are from a million dollar&nbsp;
household. So, join me as we take a look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.720
history of being a milllioniare and why modern&nbsp;
day millionaires just don’t feel the same.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:09.600
THE BIRTH OF MILLIONAIRES:
Being a millionaire probably seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.000
like a super American capitalist ideology but&nbsp;
the term actually dates back to the early 1700s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.760
to France though it did involve America. France&nbsp;
was in a pretty sticky financial situation at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.520
time as the probability of bankruptcy was becoming&nbsp;
larger and larger with each passing day. Instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:31.080
of just cutting back on spending though, the Duke&nbsp;
of Orleans decided that it would be a great idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:37.440
to embrace a more modern solution: aka take on a&nbsp;
bunch of debt. Essentially, they would open a sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.600
of national bank and transfer all of the country’s&nbsp;
debt to the bank. And to pay back this debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:48.420
France was going to open up the Mississippi Valley&nbsp;
for trade and mining. On paper, this was a solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.280
plan as the Mississippi Valley was not only in&nbsp;
a prime location but it was apparently mineral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.780
rich. Not wanting to miss out, the French would&nbsp;
begin buying shares of the Mississippi Company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:05.040
like there was no tomorrow, sending the stock&nbsp;
soaring from 500 livres to 18,000 livres. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.200
many of these investors would become extremely&nbsp;
rich on paper leading to the coining of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.200
term "millionaire" in reference to people having&nbsp;
millions of livres. Unfortunately though, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.880
high didn’t last that long as the Mississippi&nbsp;
Company’s profits never materialized and the stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:23.400
would collapse soon after. Since then, we saw some&nbsp;
references to the world millionaire here and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.440
from people like Thomas Jefferson but the modern&nbsp;
capitalist millionaire didn’t pop up till the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:34.260
1800s during the gilded age. The gilded age was&nbsp;
one of the most prosperous times in US history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.420
atleast for the rich. In fact, the rich&nbsp;
were wealthier than ever, far wealthier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.100
than even the top billionaires today. These&nbsp;
industrialists owned massive oil companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:50.100
steel companies, banks, and automotive companies.&nbsp;
And these businesses were so dominant that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:55.320
often controlled over 90% of their respective&nbsp;
industries. The owners of these mega monopolies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.720
were referred to as millionaires but they weren’t&nbsp;
really millionaires. They weren’t really even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.180
billionaires. Their wealth was best described in&nbsp;
the trillions. They controlled several percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:11.640
points of the entire American GDP which translates&nbsp;
to each of them boasting net worths of a quarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.740
trillion or a half a trillion. So, originally,&nbsp;
the term millionaire wasn’t used to describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:22.320
people with a million dollars per se. Rather, it&nbsp;
was used to describe the Elon Musks and Jeff Bezos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.840
of the world. But the significance of the world&nbsp;
millionaire has only gone downhill since then.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.600
It’s easy to just point at inflation and say that&nbsp;
that’s what devalued millionaires but that’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:40.080
really the case. A million 1880 dollars is only&nbsp;
worth $30 million today, not hundreds of billions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.360
So, the culprit isn’t inflation. Rather, the real&nbsp;
culprit is actually economic growth. Back in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.640
late 1800s, the US didn’t measure GDP but it’s&nbsp;
estimated to be somewhere between $10 and $20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:59.400
billion. Today, the American GDP stands at $26.5&nbsp;
trillion or 1000 to 2000 times more. So, really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.680
whenever you see the word millionaire back in the&nbsp;
late 1800s and early 1900s, you can just replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.680
it with the world billionaire because that’s&nbsp;
really what being a millionaire originally was.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:16.140
THE GOLDEN PERIOD:
Clearly, becoming a millionaire was completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:20.340
unreasonable for the average person during the&nbsp;
gilded age and this only became worse during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:25.680
the great depression but things would bounce back&nbsp;
stronger than anyone thought possible after World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.560
War 2. The 1960s brought with it likely the&nbsp;
strongest economic period in US history from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:37.620
the average person’s perspective. Unemployment&nbsp;
was consistently in the 3 to 5% range and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.540
is when the idea of the American dream really&nbsp;
started to flourish. People could come to America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:48.000
start a business, and become a millionaire not by&nbsp;
the age of 25 or something but maybe throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.180
an entire lifetime. This was also likely the&nbsp;
best time to aim to become a millionaire. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.500
wasn’t super out there like trying to become a&nbsp;
billionaire or something. But it also wasn’t super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.060
common like the average price of a Californian&nbsp;
house nowadays. It was akin to trying to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:08.880
around $10 million today which sounds about&nbsp;
right. A hard and ambitious goal for sure but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:15.180
also not too out there. But this golden age&nbsp;
of millionaires didn’t last forever. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:21.120
it didn’t even last 10 years as the 1970s brought&nbsp;
with it an extraordinary amount of pain. Right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.100
at the beginning of the decade, President Richard&nbsp;
Nixon would put an end to the Bretton Woods System&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:32.460
aka the Gold Standard. This meant that US dollars&nbsp;
would no longer be backed by gold which led much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:38.460
of the general public losing trust in the dollar.&nbsp;
And as trust plummeted, inflation skyrocketed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:44.340
and this was much worse than what we saw over the&nbsp;
past few years. Back in the 1970s, inflation would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.800
peak above 10% on two separate occasions. To make&nbsp;
things worse, the Middle Eastern Countries would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.600
ban together and launch an oil strike against&nbsp;
America leading to one of the worst energy crises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:00.480
of all time. To put things in perspective, within&nbsp;
just these 10 years, the value of the dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:07.080
would over halve while the median price of a home&nbsp;
nearly tripled. Things were getting pretty bad and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:13.620
American was in desperate need of a change: enter&nbsp;
Ronald Reagan. The landmark of Reagan’s presidency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:19.680
was no doubt Reagonomics. Now, you can get into&nbsp;
weeds of all of the specifics and intracices but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:25.320
in a broad sense, Reagonomics was pretty simple.&nbsp;
Essentially, the idea was to make it easier for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:30.480
rich people to grow their wealth by cutting taxes&nbsp;
and decreasing regulation. As the rich got richer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:35.520
some of their wealth should naturally trickle&nbsp;
down to people of all classes or atleast that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:42.600
was the theory. In practice, this turned out to&nbsp;
be completely false. On one hand, this definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.920
reignited the American dream. It’s obviously a lot&nbsp;
easier to make a million dollars when you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:52.140
pay less in taxes and deal with less regulation.&nbsp;
So, the rich did indeed get richer pulling along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:57.420
the American economy with them. The problem though&nbsp;
was that not much wealth actually made it’s way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:02.760
the average American as the rich naturally just&nbsp;
tended to keep the extra money they made. If a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.980
business made an extra $100 in profit, $90 would&nbsp;
just go straight to the owner and maybe $10 would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:12.480
make it’s way back to the people. Combine this&nbsp;
with the fact that Reagan had to cut down on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:17.340
bunch of social programs to make this work and&nbsp;
suddenly the average person was worse off than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:24.860
before. But there was a select group of average&nbsp;
people that were doing better than ever before.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:30.900
TECH MILLIONAIRES:
It may seem like tech bros making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:36.300
millions is a new phenomenon thanks to companies&nbsp;
like Google, Facebook, and Apple. But, this trend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:42.360
can actually be traced back to the 1990s. Take&nbsp;
Dell for example. Following Dell’s IPO in 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:48.540
a whopping 2,700 millionaires would be minted over&nbsp;
the next 20 years. In fact, Dell millionaires were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:54.120
so common that people just referred to them as&nbsp;
dellionaires and this marked the first step of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:00.360
the normalization of millionaires. While 2,700&nbsp;
millionaires was definitely a massive amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:04.980
for a single company back in the 90s and&nbsp;
2000s, compared to the overall population,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:10.920
it was still an extremely small amount. But,&nbsp;
this was just the beginning. Keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:15.780
many of the tech companies that dominate the&nbsp;
Fortune 500 today were yet to even be created.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:22.020
Over the next 20 years, these companies would&nbsp;
grow to unimaginable scales reaching hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:27.780
of billions if not trillions in market cap.&nbsp;
And I know we talk about such numbers all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:33.960
time but let’s take a second to recognize just how&nbsp;
massive a trillion dollars is. A trillion dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:40.500
is literally a million millions. In other words, a&nbsp;
trillion dollar company can make a million people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:46.920
millionaires. Apple with their $3 trillion market&nbsp;
cap can produce 3 million millionaires. Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:52.440
2.7 million millionaires. Google, 1.5&nbsp;
million millionaires, Amazon, 1.3 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:58.020
millionaires. Nvidia, 1.1 million millionaires.&nbsp;
And that’s just the top 5 companies. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:03.480
none of these companies have actually created&nbsp;
a million millionaires yet, but what they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:08.820
done is create hundreds of thousands of employee&nbsp;
and investor millionaires each. Think about this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:14.100
8 out of the top 10 richest people in the world&nbsp;
made all of their money in tech. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:19.200
you could even classify Warren Buffett as a tech&nbsp;
billionaire given that he owns a $150 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.660
worth of Apple stock. And I imagine that this&nbsp;
is the same case with most millionaires as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:30.060
The US currently boasts a total of 22 million&nbsp;
millionaires most of whom made their money in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:35.400
tech and live in tech cities like San Francisco,&nbsp;
Seattle, and Austin. In San Francisco for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:41.580
you need to almost be a multi millionaire to even&nbsp;
feel financially comfortable. And that’s not my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:46.080
opinion. Charles Schwab actually conducted a&nbsp;
full on study about this. They found that to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:51.660
be financially comfortable in San Francisco,&nbsp;
you need a net worth of $1.7 million. To be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:57.840
considered wealthy, you need a net worth of $4.7&nbsp;
million. So, our friend from the beginning really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:01.560
wasn’t exaggerating when he said that being&nbsp;
a multi millionaire very much felt like being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:06.900
in the middle class atleast in a Californian&nbsp;
tech city. But really, it’s only a matter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:12.600
time until it becomes the same case for the rest&nbsp;
of America thanks to inflation, economic growth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:17.760
and tech’s increasing omnipresense. What started&nbsp;
off as a way to describe the richest people on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:23.640
the planet has since fallen to be a generic term&nbsp;
to describe the upper middle class atleast within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.560
America and other western countries. Of course,&nbsp;
a million dollars is still an extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.900
amount of money outside these countries. But,&nbsp;
that’s why millionaires just don’t feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:38.400
millionaires anymore. The new measure of wealth&nbsp;
seems to be earning a million dollar salary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:42.600
Check out this video to learn about ChatGPT’s&nbsp;
struggle to payout million dollar salaries.

